WEBVTT

00:00:00.675 --> 00:00:02.375
A customer checks out.

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:05.125
The shop’s web service calls its API,

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:10.225
which reaches a database server called
pg-shop through the database service.

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:12.725
The arrows show what checkout depends on.

00:00:12.725 --> 00:00:16.225
It takes several sources to piece together
that short path.

00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:19.608
Now the map pulls back.

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:20.958
At the end of this replay,

00:00:20.958 --> 00:00:25.633
Sattle’s master graph holds seventy-three
entities and sixty-nine links.

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:29.533
An entity can be a pod, a service or an
Azure resource.

00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:32.683
The links record facts such as which pods
called each other

00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:34.183
and where a pod ran.

00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:39.067
The checkout path is one view of that
larger graph.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:42.492
Sattle calls the query that builds a view
a projection.

00:00:42.492 --> 00:00:45.617
One projection maps the shop’s
dependencies.

00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:48.367
Another combines that map with meter
readings

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:51.592
and rules to show how an outage can reach
checkout.

00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:53.867
That second view is the health model.

00:00:55.825 --> 00:01:01.325
The box encloses services in the shop
namespace, a named group in Kubernetes.

00:01:01.325 --> 00:01:04.750
Its database runs outside that box on
Azure.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.675
The scope says which services belong to
the shop.

00:01:07.675 --> 00:01:10.475
Their dependencies can still lead outside
it.

00:01:12.008 --> 00:01:15.733
For pg-shop, one bad minute opens the
alarm.

00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:17.908
The first good minute keeps it open.

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:19.458
The second closes it.

00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:22.108
That memory has a name, hysteresis.

00:01:22.108 --> 00:01:24.658
People can choose and refine rules like
this

00:01:24.658 --> 00:01:29.133
to express what counts as failure and
recovery for a particular service.

00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:34.083
The API needs the database to finish
checkout.

00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:38.758
A cache could be optional if the API can
serve the request without it.

00:01:38.758 --> 00:01:41.408
A network record shows that a call
happened.

00:01:41.408 --> 00:01:43.333
The health rules decide whether trouble at

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:46.033
the far end should count against the
caller.

00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:50.967
One report lists pg-shop in Azure.

00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:53.217
Another carries its meter reading.

00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:55.967
Both contain the same resource ID.

00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:59.217
That shared identity lets the model attach
a new reading

00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:01.267
to the server it already knows.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.900
Three web pods call two API pods.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.575
A pod is a running copy of a program.

00:02:08.575 --> 00:02:13.650
At the service level, those calls become
one arrow from web to API.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.525
This projection groups the callers

00:02:15.525 --> 00:02:18.375
and their targets by the service they
belong to.

00:02:19.908 --> 00:02:22.883
The database service points to pg-shop.

00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:27.183
They’re two different things, and the link
says the server backs the service.

00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:30.258
An ontology defines that vocabulary.

00:02:30.258 --> 00:02:33.008
People can maintain it as the system
grows,

00:02:33.008 --> 00:02:36.783
giving each new kind of thing and
relationship a precise meaning.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:43.367
At 10:11, the meter reports that pg-shop
stopped answering.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:46.717
The recorder gets that reading a minute
and a second later.

00:02:46.717 --> 00:02:50.717
The chart places the zero at 10:11, the
sample time.

00:02:50.717 --> 00:02:53.117
That keeps the outage in the minute it
happened.

00:02:54.683 --> 00:03:00.033
Down that column, the database service
turns red, then the API that needs it.

00:03:00.033 --> 00:03:02.983
Web and the gateway both need the API.

00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:04.758
The cache stays green.

00:03:04.758 --> 00:03:07.883
A failure can reach a caller several steps
away.

00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:11.508
The health model follows the dependencies
to find those callers.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.908
At 10:14 the database answers again.

00:03:15.908 --> 00:03:19.058
Its meter rises while its alarm stays red.

00:03:19.058 --> 00:03:21.733
At 10:15, the second good minute clears

00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:24.683
the alarm for the database and its
callers.

00:03:24.683 --> 00:03:26.808
A reading describes one moment.

00:03:26.808 --> 00:03:29.933
A health state can depend on several
readings in a row.

00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:34.917
Earlier that morning, the cache was under
heavy load.

00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:36.742
Web already depended on it.

00:03:36.742 --> 00:03:40.092
Both show amber, the warning state called
Degraded.

00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:42.542
The gateway and API were healthy.

00:03:44.075 --> 00:03:48.400
In a second run, the API’s settings name
the existing cache.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.175
Sattle matches the address to its Azure
resource and adds a dependency.

00:03:53.175 --> 00:03:57.400
That one setting is evidence for an arrow
the original run didn’t have.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.475
Both runs stop at the same time.

00:04:01.475 --> 00:04:04.750
The cache meter reads eighty-five percent
in each.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:10.150
Only the second gateway turns amber,
because its API now depends on the cache.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.425
Holding the readings and rules fixed makes

00:04:12.425 --> 00:04:14.725
the effect of that one new link visible.

00:04:16.683 --> 00:04:21.183
In another replay, the API names a new
host, offers-cache.

00:04:21.183 --> 00:04:23.083
Its address is on the left.

00:04:23.083 --> 00:04:26.233
The Azure inventory on the right has no
match yet.

00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:29.758
The setting tells us where the API intends
to connect.

00:04:29.758 --> 00:04:33.008
The resource at that address is still an
open question.

00:04:34.625 --> 00:04:39.275
Now Azure reports a Redis cache whose name
and type match that address.

00:04:39.275 --> 00:04:41.200
It appears in the overlap.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.200
A query can join the two records and
connect the API to the new resource.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.600
The same rule can recognize another cache
when it appears.

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:54.308
The new cache appears on the map with a
question mark.

00:04:54.308 --> 00:04:57.008
Azure supplied evidence that it exists.

00:04:57.008 --> 00:05:01.558
To judge its health, Sattle also needs
readings that a health rule can use.

00:05:01.558 --> 00:05:04.233
Until then, the state is Unknown.

00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:07.983
A different resource type may call for a
different rule or meter.

00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:12.483
The Canvas starts with a saved graph.

00:05:12.483 --> 00:05:17.658
As pg-shop’s state changes, the engine
sends a patch listing the edits.

00:05:17.658 --> 00:05:21.608
Here seven edits carry the map from
revision fifteen to sixteen.

00:05:21.608 --> 00:05:25.408
A gap in reporting leaves the last known
resource in place.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.600
Revision sixteen shows the outage.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.275
By eighteen the database has recovered.

00:05:32.275 --> 00:05:34.775
A revision counts changes to the graph,

00:05:34.775 --> 00:05:36.875
so the distance between those numbers says

00:05:36.875 --> 00:05:39.325
nothing about how many minutes passed.

00:05:39.325 --> 00:05:41.250
The meter keeps time in minutes.

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:46.258
One path applies the edits as each record
arrives.

00:05:46.258 --> 00:05:49.758
The other builds the answer again from the
same recorded inputs,

00:05:49.758 --> 00:05:51.808
clock events and rules.

00:05:51.808 --> 00:05:53.958
Both end at the same graph.

00:05:53.958 --> 00:05:56.933
That agreement makes a past result
reproducible

00:05:56.933 --> 00:05:59.433
and gives Sattle a way to test its
updates.

00:06:01.392 --> 00:06:04.017
At revision sixteen, web is red.

00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:09.717
Its dependency path leads through the API
and the database service to pg-shop.

00:06:09.717 --> 00:06:13.167
There, a meter reading tripped the
server’s own alarm.

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:16.142
The Canvas marks that server as an origin.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.842
Web shows the effect.

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:22.942
Why does the database service point to
that particular server?

00:06:22.942 --> 00:06:26.067
The health view takes the link from the
structure query.

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:30.017
That query matched the service’s host name
to the Azure resource.

00:06:30.017 --> 00:06:32.692
The white paper calls this trail lineage.

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:35.867
Each step leads back to the records behind
the result.

00:06:37.383 --> 00:06:40.258
The Canvas can also preview a different
rule.

00:06:40.258 --> 00:06:42.758
A draft runs against the recorded case

00:06:42.758 --> 00:06:46.433
and shows which rows it returns before the
rule changes.

00:06:46.433 --> 00:06:49.308
The records still describe the same
outage.

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:52.883
A different answer shows which conclusion
depends on the rule.
